# rule.require.a-cue-is-not-a-claim

> `declare-once` binds a CLAIM, not a CUE. a costly defect earns as many cues as it takes, from as many angles as you can find.

claim = asserts a fact a reader may act on. cue = a detector (when-row, smell, symptom, a rule aimed at one defect from one angle).

test: *could these two passages DISAGREE?* yes → claims: declare one, cite it · no → cues: keep both, look for a third.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you would cut a when-row because a peer row covers it | could they disagree? no → keep both |
| you author a second or third rule on one defect | correct where each grades a different property; name the seam |
| you would restate a measurement or count in a summary | that is a claim: cite it |
| a cue cites a fact (number, file, incident) | the cue is exempt; the fact inside obeys `declare-once` |
| you cannot name the angle a new cue adds | cut it |

blocker: a cue cut on `declare-once` grounds where the two could not disagree · a claim (count, measurement, verdict) restated where a citation would serve.
nitpick: a new rule on an extant defect with no named angle.
